-- title:
--   Localised reduction agrees with coefficientwise reduction
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a commutative ring, $A \subseteq L$ a subring, $k$ a field and $\mathrm{red} : A \to k$ a ring homomorphism, and let $N$ be a natural number with $N \neq 0$. Write $R =$ `modularRing N A` for the subring of the Laurent series ring $L((q))$ generated by the constant series attached to elements of $A$ together with the two series `jqModC L` and `jqNModC L N`, and let `modularLocalized N A red` be `localizedAtKer` for this $R$, that is, the subring of $L((q))$ consisting of those series $f$ for which there are $r, s \in R$ with $s$ outside the prime ideal `redKer` of $R$ and $f \cdot s = r$. On it lives the ring homomorphism `modularRedLocHom N A red` to $k((q))$, namely `redLocHom` for this $R$, whose underlying map is `redLoc`. Let $f$ be an element of `modularLocalized N A red` and assume $hf$: the underlying Laurent series of $f$ lies in `integralCoeffs A`, i.e. every coefficient $f_n$ ($n \in \mathbb{Z}$) belongs to $A$. The conclusion is that `modularRedLocHom N A red f` equals `coeffRed A red` applied to $f$ together with this integrality witness, where `coeffRed A red` is the ring homomorphism `integralCoeffs A` $\to k((q))$ obtained by viewing a coefficientwise integral series as a series over $A$ and then applying $\mathrm{red}$ to each coefficient.
--
--   This is the compatibility statement between the two reductions available on the modular Laurent-series ring of level $N$: the reduction defined by localising at the kernel prime, and the naive coefficientwise reduction, which is only defined on series whose coefficients are all integral. It is used wherever the reduction of an explicitly given $q$-expansion has to be computed coefficient by coefficient, for instance in the analysis of multiplicative coverings and of the behaviour of charts at the cusp in characteristic $p$.
